WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:02.900
- We're told, divide the polynomials.

00:02.900 --> 00:05.230
The form of your answer should either be

00:05.230 --> 00:06.620
just a clean polynomial,

00:06.620 --> 00:10.470
or some polynomial plus some
constant over x plus two,

00:10.470 --> 00:13.310
where p of x is a polynomial
and k is an integer.

00:13.310 --> 00:15.700
Fair enough, and if we were
doing this on Khan Academy,

00:15.700 --> 00:17.700
this is a screen shot from Khan Academy,

00:17.700 --> 00:18.820
we would have to type this in,

00:18.820 --> 00:20.220
but we're just going to do it by hand.

00:20.220 --> 00:22.290
And, like always, pause this video

00:22.290 --> 00:23.470
and try to do it on your own

00:23.470 --> 00:26.053
before we work through it together.

00:26.960 --> 00:28.490
All right, now let's
work through it together.

00:28.490 --> 00:32.890
And what we're trying to
do is divide x plus two,

00:32.890 --> 00:37.340
into three x to the third power

00:37.340 --> 00:42.290
plus four x squared
minus three x plus seven.

00:42.290 --> 00:43.940
And so, like always,

00:43.940 --> 00:47.650
we focus on the highest
degree terms first.

00:47.650 --> 00:50.470
x goes into three x to the
third power how many times?

00:50.470 --> 00:52.070
Three x squared times.

00:52.070 --> 00:55.200
We'd want to put that in
the second degree column.

00:55.200 --> 00:56.940
Three x squared.

00:56.940 --> 01:00.370
Three x squared times
two is six x squared.

01:00.370 --> 01:04.900
Three x squared times x is
three x to the third power.

01:04.900 --> 01:06.130
There's something very meditative

01:06.130 --> 01:07.870
about algebraic long division.

01:07.870 --> 01:10.180
Anyway, we'd want to
subtract what we just wrote

01:10.180 --> 01:11.750
from what we have up here.

01:11.750 --> 01:13.260
So let's subtract.

01:13.260 --> 01:15.810
And these characters cancel out.

01:15.810 --> 01:18.650
And then four x squared
minus six x squared

01:18.650 --> 01:21.620
is negative two x squared.

01:21.620 --> 01:24.510
Bring down that negative three x.

01:24.510 --> 01:26.410
And now we would wanna say,

01:26.410 --> 01:30.960
hey, how many times does x go
into negative two x squared?

01:30.960 --> 01:33.500
Well, it would go negative two x times.

01:33.500 --> 01:35.660
Put that in our first degree column.

01:35.660 --> 01:39.900
Negative two x times
two is negative four x.

01:39.900 --> 01:44.470
Negative two x times x is
negative two x squared.

01:44.470 --> 01:46.750
Now we wanna subtract what
we have here in orange

01:46.750 --> 01:48.940
from what we have up here in teal.

01:48.940 --> 01:51.940
So we either put a negative
around the whole thing

01:51.940 --> 01:54.680
or we distribute that negative
and that becomes a positive,

01:54.680 --> 01:56.560
that becomes a positive.

01:56.560 --> 02:00.000
And so this is equal to, the
x squared terms cancel out.

02:00.000 --> 02:01.930
Negative three x plus four x

02:01.930 --> 02:04.260
is just going to be a straight up x.

02:04.260 --> 02:07.920
Bring down that seven, x plus seven.

02:07.920 --> 02:10.980
How many times does x go into x?

02:10.980 --> 02:12.360
Well, one time.

02:12.360 --> 02:14.580
Actually, let me do a,
use a new color here.

02:14.580 --> 02:16.910
So, how many times does x go into x?

02:16.910 --> 02:18.540
It goes one time.

02:18.540 --> 02:20.520
Put that in the constant column.

02:20.520 --> 02:22.270
One times two is two.

02:22.270 --> 02:23.770
One times x is x.

02:23.770 --> 02:25.730
We wanna subtract these characters.

02:25.730 --> 02:30.730
And we're left with
seven minus two is five.

02:30.900 --> 02:35.900
And so, we can rewrite
this whole thing as,

02:36.160 --> 02:39.120
we deserve I guess a
little bit of a drum roll.

02:39.120 --> 02:43.640
Three x squared minus two x plus one,

02:43.640 --> 02:46.360
plus the remainder,

02:46.360 --> 02:49.140
five, over x plus two.

02:49.140 --> 02:50.210
One way to think about it is,

02:50.210 --> 02:51.220
hey, I have this remainder,

02:51.220 --> 02:53.790
I'd have to keep dividing it by x plus two

02:53.790 --> 02:56.510
if I really wanted to figure
out exactly what this is.

02:56.510 --> 02:59.720
Now if I wanted these expressions
to be completely identical

02:59.720 --> 03:02.820
I would put a condition on the domain

03:02.820 --> 03:05.340
that x cannot be equal to negative two

03:05.340 --> 03:07.030
because if x was equal to negative two,

03:07.030 --> 03:09.060
we'd be dividing by zero here.

03:09.060 --> 03:11.210
But for the purposes of this exercise,

03:11.210 --> 03:16.140
you just have to input
this part right over here,

03:16.140 --> 03:17.400
you'd have to type it in,

03:17.400 --> 03:19.730
which I guess isn't the easiest
thing to do in the world.

03:19.730 --> 03:21.630
But it's worth doing.

03:21.630 --> 03:23.480
All right, see you in the next video.
